# What NOT to upload as build input.
#
# Vercel serves only `dist/` (outputDirectory in vercel.json), so nothing listed
# here was ever reachable by a visitor. This keeps the upload to what
# `node build.mjs` actually reads.

# Built here by the build command, so shipping a local copy only risks a stale one.
dist/
node_modules/

# Editor and agent state.
.claude/
.vscode/

# Docs — read by people, not by the build.
README.md

# sql/ is NOT ignored. It used to be, back when the schema was one file only
# people read. The build command now runs tools/check.mjs, which reads every
# numbered file in sql/ to prove each form field still has a column — so it is
# a build input, and ignoring it makes the deploy fail on a missing file
# rather than pass.
#
# Its filled-in copies are a different matter. A *.local.sql file is a
# migration with the real WEBHOOK_SECRET pasted over the placeholder, made to
# be pasted into Supabase and then deleted. .gitignore keeps them out of git,
# but a `vercel` deploy from this folder takes its list from this file and
# cannot be relied on to read .gitignore as well — so without this line a CLI
# deploy, preview included, could store the secret in the deployment's source
# for anybody with the project to read. (A deploy from git never had them:
# they were never committed.) build.mjs and check.mjs already skip them, so nothing needs
# them. The same goes for the local env files the tools read, which can hold
# keys stronger than that one.
*.local.sql
.env*

# What tools/walk-paying.mjs --go leaves behind when a run is killed: one
# machine's state, not the project's.
tools/.walk-made.json

# The Apache config. Vercel reads vercel.json and never this, and build.mjs
# does not copy it into dist/ — it is kept in the repo for a move to Apache
# hosting, and copied up by hand if that ever happens. See the file itself.
.htaccess
